{"name":"Glass Bead","hex":"#c7bec4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"},{"term":"bead","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Prayer, later especially with a rosary. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Each in a string of small balls making up the rosary or paternoster."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into a bead.","To apply beads to."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bead"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glass Bead","blocks":[{"text":"\"Glass Bead\" is the debut single by South Korean girl group GFriend. It was released by Source Music on January 15, 2015, as the lead single of their debut extended play, Season of Glass (2015). Written and produced by Iggy and Youngbae, the song was described by Fuse as a \"nostalgic gem that heavily recalls Girls' Generation's earliest work, from the sound to the energetic choreography\"."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_Bead","wikidata":"Q22974625"},{"title":"Glass bead making","blocks":[{"text":"Glass bead making has long traditions, with the oldest known beads dating over 3,000 years. People have been making beads out of glass since at least Ancient Roman times. Perhaps the earliest glass-like beads were Egyptian faience beads, a form of clay bead with a self-forming vitreous coating. Glass beads are significant in archaeology because the presence of glass beads often indicates that there was trade and that the bead making technology was being spread. In addition, the composition of the glass beads could be analyzed and help archaeologists understand the sources of the beads."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Lampworking-closeup.jpg/330px-Lampworking-closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_bead_making","wikidata":"Q1435588"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass","wikidata":"Q11469"},{"title":"Bead","blocks":[{"text":"A bead is a small, decorative object that is formed in a variety of shapes and sizes of a material such as stone, bone, shell, glass, plastic, wood, or pearl and with a small hole for threading or stringing. Beads range in size from under 1 mm to over 1 cm in diameter."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/Beads.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bead","wikidata":"Q1053956"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Lampwork2.jpg/500px-Lampwork2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Lampwork2.jpg/1280px-Lampwork2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Lampwork2.jpg/500px-Lampwork2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lampwork glass beads","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALampwork2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pressedglass.jpg/500px-Pressedglass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pressedglass.jpg/500px-Pressedglass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pressed glass beads","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APressedglass.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Dichroicclose.jpg/500px-Dichroicclose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Dichroicclose.jpg/500px-Dichroicclose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lampworked dichroic glass bead showing thin film application","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADichroicclose.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Furnaceglass.jpg/500px-Furnaceglass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Furnaceglass.jpg/500px-Furnaceglass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Furnace glass beads","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFurnaceglass.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Necklace_3_-_Rus%27_12-13c_-_Museum_of_Archaeology_of_Moscow.jpg/500px-Necklace_3_-_Rus%27_12-13c_-_Museum_of_Archaeology_of_Moscow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Necklace_3_-_Rus%27_12-13c_-_Museum_of_Archaeology_of_Moscow.jpg/1280px-Nec